< Task-2 >
Edit the YAML to set strategy.rollingUpdate to maxSurge: 1 and maxUnavailable: 0. Add a readiness probe (HTTP GET / on port 80). Apply it.

< Strategy >

# Controls how old pods are replaced on a pod template change.
# RollingUpdate (default): gradual, no downtime. Recreate: kill all, then start; downtime.
# maxSurge: extra pods above desired. maxUnavailable: pods allowed below desired. Default 25%/25%. Both can’t be 0.
# Production: maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0 (capacity never dips).

< yaml for strategy >

strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: 1
    maxUnavailable: 0

< readinessProbe >

# Decides if a pod gets traffic. Fail = removed from Service endpoints, not restarted.
# Rollouts wait for it, so a bad release stalls while old pods keep serving.
# No probe = Ready the moment the container starts, so traffic can hit a broken app.

< yaml for readinessProbe >

readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /
    port: 80
  initialDelaySeconds: 3
  periodSeconds: 5

Interview answer:

The default 25%/25% is acceptable for small or non-critical services, but for production I set maxSurge and maxUnavailable explicitly, usually maxUnavailable: 0, and pair them with readiness probes, so capacity never drops and a bad release stalls instead of causing an outage

